Log file is a type of text file used to record events or activity automatically
Common log files
sys log - general system logs
auth.log - login/authorization log
boot.log

/var/log displays all log files 
When you are in auth.log file, you are able to see all login attempts and error messages 

-f means follow mode and allows you to see real time updates
eg. tail -f /var/log/auth.log

Journalctl is used to view system logs of a service
eg. journalctl -u nginx (show logs only for nginx)
journalctl -u nginx -n 20 (show last 20 lines)
journalctl -n nginx -f (to see live view, see errors as they happen)

A service is a program that runs in the background continuously(not like app that open & close) eg. ngix, ssh, docker etc
systemd manages and controls services(with this, everything is automated)

systemctl is the command used to talk to systemd
eg. sudo systemct1 start nginx (turn on a service)
sudo systemct1 stop nginx (turn off a service)
sudo systemct1 restart nginx (restart a service)
systemctl status nginx (shows status of a service whether it is running or not, logs, PID, errors)


SSH(Secure Shell) allows you connect to another server securely
To connect to another server ssh username@ip-address

If unable to connect to ssh because of a different port, troubleshoot with this
ssh -p (port number) username@ip-address (port number adds some kind of security, it tells which service to talk to)




